WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Instructor] One of the
chief responsibilities

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.790
of the U.S. government is
protecting the rights of citizens.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.150
But what are those rights?

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.150
The extent of and limits on
rights can be very complex.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.960
That's why we have constitutional lawyers

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.530
and Supreme Court cases

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.060
to decide whether the government protects

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.943
or prohibits certain activities.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.760
But we can make some generalizations

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.630
about categories of rights in
the American political system.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.340
In this and the videos that
follow, we're gonna distinguish

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.330
between three different types of rights,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.133
personal rights, political
rights, and economic rights.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.410
So let's dive in to personal rights.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.210
This is a really big umbrella

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.470
that includes individual's right

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.830
to decide the best course
of action for themselves.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.520
Personal rights define the
ways that you should be free

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.690
from government interference
in your private life,

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:09.090
your home, your mind, and your body.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.450
You'll recognize some of
them from the Bill of Rights,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.780
particularly the First Amendment,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.300
which protects freedom
of speech and religion,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.200
and the Fourth Amendment,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.630
which protects the right
of the people to be secure

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:28.560
in their persons, houses,
papers, and effects

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.453
against unreasonable
searches and seizures.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.730
So what are some of these rights?

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.900
I've tried to put them into a spectrum

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.900
from freedom of body to
freedom of mind and heart,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.150
although you'll see that
there are lots of places

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.250
where freedom of body and
freedom of mind overlap.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.570
First, there's freedom of movement.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.900
This means that you can
move to a different state

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.180
or even move to a different
country if you want to.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.620
The U.S. government won't
force you to stay put.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.070
There's the freedom of association.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.823
That is to hang out
with anyone you want to.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.040
There's the freedom to
refuse medical care,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.490
to be able to control your own body.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.890
The government can't force you

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.910
to undergo a medical procedure.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.580
Likewise, there's the
freedom to have children,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.150
as many or as few as you want.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.680
There's also the freedom

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.470
from government intrusion
into your private domain,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.500
also known as the right to privacy.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.450
It means that the government can't come

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.230
into your home without a
warrant or otherwise interfere

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.050
in your private life behind closed doors.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.650
There's the freedom of expression,

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.740
the ability of an individual or group

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.343
to express their beliefs,
thoughts, ideas, and emotions.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:00.140
There's the freedom to access education.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:04.700
So people who are school
age have the right

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.040
to receive a public education
no matter who they are

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.623
or whether they have learning differences.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.763
There's the freedom to
marry whomever you choose.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:21.480
And lastly, there's freedom
of religion and conscience.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.050
This is your personal right

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:26.600
to think and believe whatever you want.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.170
The United States doesn't
have an established church,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.040
meaning one that the
government supports financially

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.863
or that citizens are obligated to attend.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.110
Some of these freedoms
might seem like no-brainers,

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.370
but people who live in
authoritarian societies

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.350
don't necessarily enjoy these same rights.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:51.190
If you've ever read George
Orwell's novel "1984,"

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.010
it's a good example of what
society might look like

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.770
without these rights.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:59.760
In it, the government spies
on everyone in their homes

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.860
and prevents anyone from speaking out

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.020
against the ruling party.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.200
But it's also important to recognize

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.320
that these rights aren't absolute.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:10.960
Many of them have limits,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.980
and what those limits
should be sparks a lot

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.740
of public debate.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.150
For example, when we talk about the right

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.760
to refuse medical care, we
might think about vaccines.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.690
Should people be required to get vaccines

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.910
if not doing so poses a risk
to the health of others?

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.540
What about freedom of expression?

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.610
Should people be able to
say whatever they want,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.520
or should the government
impose limits on hate speech?

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.750
The personal rights of one
person will frequently overlap

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:48.463
with or even contradict the
personal rights of someone else.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.770
So that's it for this brief
overview of personal rights.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:54.730
In the next video,

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.513
we'll discuss the political
rights of citizens.

